-- GAME ----------------------------------------------------------------------
  Title       Quazatron
  Publisher   Hewson, 1986
  Version     1.0

-- YOU NEED ------------------------------------------------------------------
  Your own copy of the original game, saved beside this patch:

  File        Quazatron (1986)(Hewson Consultants)[aka Ziggurat].z80
  MD5         ac51eb58af87fdac9c0c7077db98c90a

  The patch carries none of the game's code. It only works with that exact
  dump, under that name or any other: anything else is refused, with a
  message naming the file it wants.

-- WHAT IT DOES --------------------------------------------------------------
  Moving figures become Next hardware sprites, so they keep their own colours
  and no longer take on the scenery's (colour clash). Game logic is unchanged.

  Colours     Default (the game's own) plus: Colour, chosen on the menu, off
              by default.
  Enhanced    The monochrome decks in colour: floors, walls, ramps, arrow
              panels, objects and pads each in their own tone, taken from the
              deck's own colour; every droid painted by its parts as a
              hardware sprite, the player's droid included. The panel below
              the play area is unchanged. A painted set, colourist not named.

  Pace        original

  Changes     1.0: colour scenery on Layer 2 and droids as painted sprites, as
              the Colour set over a monochrome base; Smooth scrolling as a
              menu toggle; the original's pace kept by a pace limiter (hook
              time repaid from the game's own delay, on each scroll move, and
              in pace windows on the game's block blits and scroll-step copy)

  Note        Kempston: choose 4 on CONTROL SELECTION, ENTER, then fire.
              Colour is the menu's Enhanced colours line: on, the colour game;
              off, the game's own monochrome picture (the patched game, at the
              original's pace). The main line off in the loader gives your own
              unpatched game. The menu .nex starts with Colour on. Smooth
              scrolling (off by default) makes scroll bursts quicker and
              glides the view; it has no effect in monochrome. Scenery is a
              wrapping Layer 2 canvas coloured by the game's own drawing
              lists; droids are Next sprites painted by parts. Pace measured
              in JNext only; hardware untested.

